Dirty read: Read uncommitted data | dirty read
Non repeatable read: Read committed data | no dirty read but non-repeatable read
Repeatable read: Read repeatable data | no dirty read and no non-repeatable read


@Transaction isolation level
Problem                             What Happens                                    Isolation Level to Prevent
Dirty Read                          Reads uncommitted data                          READ COMMITTED (NoSQL, elasticSearch)
Non-Repeatable Read                 Same row read twice → different data            REPEATABLE READ (DEFAULT for MYSQL)
Phantom Read                        Same query → different row count                SERIALIZABLE

🔹 1. Dirty Read
Definition:
When a transaction reads uncommitted (temporary) data from another ongoing transaction.

Example:
	•	T1: Updates a customer’s balance from 1000 → 500 (but hasn’t committed).
	•	T2: Reads the new balance (500) before T1 commits.
	•	If T1 later rolls back, T2 has read data that never actually existed → a dirty read.

How to Avoid:
	•	Use READ COMMITTED or higher isolation level.
	•	Most databases (like Oracle, PostgreSQL) default to this to prevent dirty reads.

⸻

🔹 2. Non-Repeatable Read
Definition:
When a transaction reads the same row twice and gets different values because another transaction modified and committed it in between.

Example:
	•	T1: Reads customer balance = 1000.
	•	T2: Updates balance = 500 and commits.
	•	T1: Reads the same balance again = 500.
→ T1 got different results in the same transaction — a non-repeatable read.

How to Avoid:
	•	Use REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE isolation level.
	•	Lock the row for the duration of the transaction (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE).

⸻

🔹 3. Phantom Read

Definition:
When a transaction re-executes a query returning a set of rows and finds new or missing rows due to another transaction’s insert/delete.

Example:
	•	T1: Executes SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE amount > 1000 → returns 5 rows.
	•	T2: Inserts a new order with amount = 1500 and commits.
	•	T1: Executes the same query again → now returns 6 rows.
→ The new “phantom” row causes a phantom read.

How to Avoid:
	•	Use SERIALIZABLE isolation level (highest level).
	•	Database ensures transactions run as if they were serialized — no new rows can appear mid-transaction.


SQL query to return all the jobs that are in-progress from job table and tasks table based on start time and end time
SELECT
    j.job_id,
    j.job_name,
    j.start_time AS job_start,
    j.end_time AS job_end,
    t.task_id,
    t.task_name,
    t.start_time AS task_start,
    t.end_time AS task_end
FROM job j
JOIN task t ON j.job_id = t.job_id
WHERE
    -- Job in progress
    j.start_time <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    AND (j.end_time IS NULL OR j.end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)

    -- Task in progress
    AND t.start_time <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    AND (t.end_time IS NULL OR t.end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);



