ConcurrentHashMap vs HashTable vs SynchronizedMap
ConcurrentHashMap:
	- lock striping at segment level
	- better performance
	- null key and null value not allowed
	- iterator is weakly consistent but not fail-fast
	- no blocking
HashTable:
	- locking at method level
	- poor performance
	- null key and null value not allowed
	- iterator is fail-fast
	- blocking
SynchronizedMap:
	- locking at object level
	- poor performance
	- null key and null value allowed
	- iterator is fail-fast
	- blocking

Fail-fast vs Fail-safe
Fail-fast:
	- iterator is fail-fast
	- iterator throws ConcurrentModificationException
	- works on the original collection
	import java.util.*;

    public class FailFastExample {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
            list.add("A");
            list.add("B");
            list.add("C");

            Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
            while (it.hasNext()) {
                String value = it.next();
                System.out.println(value);
                // Structural modification during iteration
                if (value.equals("B")) {
                    list.remove(value); // ❌ Throws ConcurrentModificationException
                    it.remove(); // ✅ Safe removal through iterator
                }
            }
        }
    }

Fail-safe:
	- iterator is fail-safe
	- iterator does not throw ConcurrentModificationException
	- works on the copy/snapshot of the collection

    public class FailSafeExample {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Map<String, String> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
            map.put("1", "A");
            map.put("2", "B");
            map.put("3", "C");

            for (String key : map.keySet()) {
                System.out.println(key + " = " + map.get(key));
                map.put("4", "D"); // ✅ No exception (Fail-Safe)
            }
        }
    }



