	🌀 DStreams (Discretized Streams) — the classic model
    	•	Spark chops incoming data into small batches (say, every 2 seconds).
    	•	Each batch becomes an RDD, and your transformations (map, filter, etc.) run on those RDDs just like in batch Spark.
    	•	After processing, results are pushed to sinks (files, databases, dashboards).
    	•	Spark keeps track of processed batches via checkpointing, ensuring recovery after failure.

    In short:

    DStreams treat the data stream as a sequence of mini-batches of RDDs. Each batch is processed independently — like watching a movie frame by frame.

	⚡ Structured Streaming — the modern engine
    	•	Spark treats streaming data as a continuously growing table.
    	•	Each new event (or batch) is just a new row appended to that table.
    	•	You write queries on that table using DataFrame or SQL syntax — Spark handles batching, offsets, and recovery automatically.
    	•	It supports event-time windows, watermarks, and exactly-once semantics out of the box.

    In short:

    Structured Streaming sees the stream as a live table — your query runs continuously, and Spark updates results as new rows arrive.

