World wide ecommerce project
	- How to do maintain the data consistency across the globe
	A. Use Distributed Databases / Global Data Stores
    	•	Examples: Cassandra, DynamoDB Global Tables, CockroachDB, Google Spanner
    	•	Features:
            •	Multi-region replication
            •	Tunable consistency (strong / eventual)
            •	Conflict resolution

    Example:
    	•	Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables replicates tables across regions.
    	•	You can choose eventual consistency for non-critical data (product catalog) and strong consistency for inventory or payment data.

    ⸻

    B. Event-Driven Architecture
    	•	Use Kafka / Pulsar / Kinesis for real-time event streaming.
    	•	Events are published (e.g., OrderCreated, InventoryUpdated) and consumed by services in other regions.
    	•	Ensures eventual consistency across services and regions.
    	Region A: Order placed → Inventory reduced locally → Publish event to Kafka
        Region B: Consume event → Update regional cache / global view

    C. Distributed Transactions / Saga Pattern
        •	Two-phase commit (2PC) is slow and not ideal globally.
        •	Prefer Saga pattern for distributed transactions:
        •	Each service performs a local transaction and publishes an event.
        •	Compensating transactions are used to rollback if needed.

        Example:
            1.	Customer orders a product in the US region.
            2.	Inventory service in US reserves stock.
            3.	Payment service confirms payment.
            4.	Shipping service schedules shipment.
            5.	If any step fails, compensating transactions are triggered (refund, release inventory).
        ⸻
        E. Idempotency Operations & Conflict Resolution(version number)
        	•	All operations should be idempotent, especially inventory updates and payments.
        	•	Use timestamps or version numbers to resolve conflicts across regions.
       ⸻
        D. Caching & Read Replicas
        	•	Use regional caches (Redis / Memcached) and read replicas to serve users fast.
        	•	Writes go to the primary region; replicas update asynchronously.
        	•	Tradeoff: slightly stale reads are acceptable for non-critical data (like product views)


